    "data": {
      "A": "Research a cache-efficient serial integer-sort alternative. Compare stable LSD radix schedules of four 8-bit passes and 11/11/10-bit passes. Preserve signed-int ordering with an appropriate sign-bit key transformation; do not assume every generated value is strictly positive. Tune histogram initialization and scatter locality, accounting for scratch allocation, final-buffer ownership and any copy-back traffic. Use overflow-safe sizes and retain a correct allocation-failure fallback. Initially leave generation, validation and MD5 unchanged to isolate sorting gains. Deliver the best fully measured program, not merely the fastest isolated sorting kernel.",
      "B": "Research robust comparison sorting and coarse four-core parallelism. Replace floating-point random pivot selection with deterministic median-based selection, then compare efficient two-way/block partitioning with duplicate-aware partitioning. Add small-range insertion sorting, bounded stack growth and an introspective fallback. Parallelize only sufficiently large independent partitions, with at most four workers and a serial path for small inputs. Do not share the global pivot PRNG between workers. Use only threading facilities supported by the unchanged build; retain a useful serial implementation otherwise. Measure partition quality, task overhead and whole-program performance rather than assuming four cores imply fourfold speedup.",
      "C": "Research exact generation and validation improvements that can complement either sorting tree. Preserve the unsigned recurrences, signed/abs behavior and floating-point conversion order: drand(0, INT_MAX) must not simply be assumed equivalent to the raw generator result. Investigate local-state generation and exact parallel block generation. For each recurrence s'=a*(s & 65535)+(s >> 16), explore jump-ahead using s_k=a^k*s_0 mod (a*65536-1), with a=18000 or 36969; establish legal-state and indexing correctness before adoption. Workers must produce consecutive portions of the original stream, not independently seeded streams. Restore the original final global state if retaining randomized quicksort. Also compare a vectorizable sortedness reduction with the original scan. Keep standard MD5 over the unchanged byte sequence; independent chunk digests are not interchangeable with it.",
      "G": "Integrate only validated programs and reusable changes produced by A/B/C. First compare each complete donor under the unchanged harness. Then measure bounded combinations: C's exact pipeline improvements with A or B, and optionally A's radix kernel as coarse leaves within B's partitioning framework. Re-evaluate allocation, memory traffic, buffer ownership, thread lifetime and small-input overhead for every combination. Preserve stdin handling, errors, validation, conditional MD5 and output formatting. Require correctness arguments beyond the four observed cases, especially for generator equivalence and signed ordering. Keep tests, oracle, compiler, flags, budgets, target and permissions unchanged. Use general workload-size thresholds, never test-identity dispatch or cached answers. Retain the fastest validated complete candidate; integration gains are not additive, and an uncombined donor may win.",
      "analysis": "This run concerns Problem B only. The baseline passed four correctness cases; its three timing samples average 71.023 ms and range from 70.685 to 71.658 ms. No phase timings or workload sizes are supplied, so bottlenecks remain hypotheses. The source exposes randomized Lomuto partitioning, floating-point pivot selection, reproducible sequential generation, a verification scan and conditional MD5. Integer radix sorting offers an algorithmic alternative; ARM64 with four CPU cores supports investigating coarse CPU parallelism, not GPU work. Generation and hashing may become limiting after sorting improves. Every candidate must actually generate and sort the original data for every valid input. Pivot choices may change, but generated values, signed ordering and final hashed bytes must remain equivalent."
    },
